Check out the PR head for issue https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104. Make a scratch directory outside the repo (mktemp -d) and create these files in it: clean1.jsonl (`{"a":1}\n{"b":2}\n`), clean2.jsonl (`[1,2]\n\n"x"\n`), bad.jsonl (`{"a":1}\n{oops}\n{"c":3}\n`, so the bad row is line 2). Let CLI=packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js, and run every command from the repo root with node. For each run, record stdout, stderr and exit code. There is one check per expect line, in order.

Check 1. Expect: "packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file> [<file> ...] checks every file named in order and prints <file>:<line>: <error> for each bad row, as for one file before".
Run: `node $CLI $D/bad.jsonl`, then `node $CLI $D/clean1.jsonl $D/bad.jsonl $D/clean2.jsonl`, then `node $CLI $D/bad.jsonl $D/clean1.jsonl $D/bad2.jsonl`, where bad2.jsonl is `nope\n` (bad row on line 1). Also run `git show <merge-base>:packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js > /tmp/old.js`, copy it next to the src folder (or run the old CLI from a checkout of the base) on $D/bad.jsonl, and compare its output with the new single-file output.
PASS: the single-file output is exactly one line `$D/bad.jsonl:2: <JSON.parse message>`, identical to the old CLI's output; the multi-file run prints that same line for bad.jsonl and nothing for the clean files; in the third run, the bad.jsonl lines come before the bad2.jsonl line (`$D/bad2.jsonl:1: ...`), following argument order.
FAIL: a bad row in any named file is not reported, output is out of order, the line format differs from `<file>:<line>: <error>`, or the single-file output differs from before.

Check 2. Expect: "A file named - is read from stdin and reported as -; a second - is a usage error (exit 2)".
Run: `node $CLI - < $D/bad.jsonl`; `node $CLI - < $D/clean1.jsonl`; `node $CLI $D/clean1.jsonl - < $D/bad.jsonl`; `node $CLI - - < $D/clean1.jsonl`; `node $CLI $D/clean1.jsonl - $D/bad.jsonl - < $D/clean1.jsonl`.
PASS: the first run prints `-:2: <error>`; the second prints nothing; the third prints `-:2: <error>`; the last two exit 2 with an error message on stderr.
FAIL: stdin is not read for `-`, it is reported under any name other than `-`, or a second `-` does not exit 2.

Check 3. Expect: "Exit 0 if every file is clean, 1 if any row in any file is bad, 2 if no file is named or any file can't be read (error on stderr, other files still checked and reported)".
Run: `node $CLI $D/clean1.jsonl $D/clean2.jsonl` (expect 0); `node $CLI $D/clean1.jsonl $D/bad.jsonl` (expect 1); `node $CLI` (expect 2, message on stderr); `node $CLI $D/clean1.jsonl $D/missing.jsonl $D/bad.jsonl` (expect 2); `node $CLI $D/missing.jsonl $D/clean1.jsonl` (expect 2).
PASS: every exit code matches; the missing-file runs put an error naming missing.jsonl on stderr, and in the first missing-file run stdout still contains `$D/bad.jsonl:2: ...`, which shows the files after the missing one were still checked and reported.
FAIL: any exit code differs, the error is missing from stderr, or files after an unreadable one are not checked or reported.

Check 4. Expect: "lint(text) in src/index.js is unchanged".
Run: `git diff <merge-base>..HEAD -- packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js` (merge-base = `git merge-base HEAD origin/main`), and `node --test packages/jsonl-lint/test/lint.test.js`.
PASS: the diff shows no change to the lint function (it is unchanged) and the lint tests pass.
FAIL: the lint function's body or signature changed.

